e:T6ee,This study examines the role of managerial structural knowledge in postacquisition integration (PAI) and its association with the retention of target firm managers. Although retaining top managers is often linked to improved acquisition outcomes, the conditions under which their retention may support integration remain underexplored. Drawing on organizational modularity theory, we theorize that structural similarity between acquiring and target firms is associated with higher managerial retention in related acquisitions. We propose that structural knowledge, defined as managers’ experience within specific organizational structures, can support PAI and is associated with improved postacquisition performance. Using a data set of 2,941 mergers and acquisitions (M&A) deals and 18,987 target firm managers (1994–2018), we find a positive relationship between structural similarity and the retention of target managers in related acquisitions. At the individual level, managers with structural knowledge aligned with the acquiring firm’s structure are more likely to be retained. Furthermore, the relationship between structural similarity and retention is stronger when the geographic distance between firms is greater or when the deal requires deep integration, as in absorption-type acquisitions. Finally, structural similarity between acquiring and target firms is positively associated with improved postacquisition performance. Our findings contribute to the M&A literature by introducing a structural knowledge perspective in understanding PAI. Our study also highlights the role of organizational modularity in M&As and advances the strategic human capital literature by identifying structural knowledge as a critical component of managerial capabilities.1:["$","div",null,{"className":"site-shell site-shell--research","children":[["$","a",null,{"className":"skip-link","href":"#main","children":"Skip to content"}],["$","header",null,{"className":"site-header","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"header-inner wrap","children":[["$","a",null,{"className":"wordmark","href":"/","aria-label":"Tingyu Du — About","children":"Tingyu Du"}],["$","nav",null,{"className":"main-nav","aria-label":"Main navigation","children":[["$","a","about",{"href":"/","aria-current":"$undefined","children":"About"}],["$","a","research",{"href":"/research/","aria-current":"page","children":"Research"}],["$","a","teaching",{"href":"/teaching/","aria-current":"$undefined","children":"Teaching"}],["$","a","cv",{"href":"/cv/","aria-current":"$undefined","children":"CV"}]]}],["$","button",null,{"className":"theme-toggle","type":"button","role":"switch","aria-label":"Dark mode","aria-checked":"false","title":"Switch to dark mode","suppressHydrationWarning":true,"children":[["$","svg",null,{"className":"theme-moon","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M20.8 13A9 9 0 0 1 11 3.2 9 9 0 1 0 20.8 13Z"}]}],["$","svg",null,{"className":"theme-sun","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","aria-hidden":"true","children":[["$","circle",null,{"cx":"12","cy":"12","r":"4"}],["$","path",null,{"d":"M12 2v2m0 16v2M2 12h2m16 0h2M4.9 4.9l1.4 1.4m11.4 11.4 1.4 1.4M4.9 19.1l1.4-1.4M17.7 6.3l1.4-1.4"}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","main",null,{"id":"main","className":"site-main","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"page-content research-page wrap","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"page-heading","children":[["$","h1",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":"Research"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"page-intro","children":"My research explores questions within and across managerial labor markets, multi-unit firm structure, and mergers and acquisitions (M&A). I examine the dynamics of managerial mobility, its role in post-M&A integration, CEO selection and succession, gender inequality in executive advancement, and the effects of artificial intelligence on managerial labor markets. Across these topics, I explore how managerial and organizational dynamics shape firm behavior and performance."}]]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"research-group","aria-labelledby":"publications-title","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"group-label","id":"publications-title","children":"Publications"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"publication-list","children":[["$","article","Post-M&A Retention of Top Managers: The Role of Structural Knowledge",{"className":"publication","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"paper-content","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/orsc.2024.18686","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":"Post-M&A Retention of Top Managers: The Role of Structural Knowledge"}]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"coauthors","children":"Du, T., & Tsolmon, U."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"paper-meta","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":["$","em",null,{"children":"Organization Science"}]}],["$","span",null,{"children":"37(3), 942–968 · 2026"}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"recognition","children":"Best Paper, STR Division · Academy of Management 2023"}],["$","details",null,{"className":"paper-abstract","children":[["$","summary",null,{"aria-label":"Abstract for Post-M&A Retention of Top Managers: The Role of Structural Knowledge","children":["Abstract ",["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":[["path",{"d":"m6 9 6 6 6-6","key":"qrunsl"}]],"className":"lucide-chevron-down","size":15,"aria-hidden":"true"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"abstract-body","children":[[["$","p","abstract",{"children":[null,"$e"]}]],"$Lf"]}]]}]]}],"$L10"]}],"$L11"]}]]}],"$L12"]}]}],"$L13"]}]
